Here is **Variant 1 — EMA Ribbon Momentum Scalp** for NQ.

**Logic summary**
- **Trend filter**: EMA(13) > EMA(21) > EMA(50) for longs (mirror for shorts)
- **Momentum**: MACD histogram > 0 *and* rising bar-over-bar (acceleration)
- **RSI guard**: RSI(14) between 40-70 for longs (avoids overbought chasing)
- **Exit**: Profit target 16 ticks (4 pts), stop loss 12 ticks (3 pts), time-stop 30 bars

```csharp
public class NQScalp_V1_EmaRibbonMomentum : Strategy
{
    private EMA ema13, ema21, ema50;
    private MACD macd;
    private RSI rsi;
    private int lastEntryBar = -100;

    [NinjaScriptProperty] public int ProfitTicks { get; set; } = 16;
    [NinjaScriptProperty] public int StopTicks { get; set; } = 12;
    [NinjaScriptProperty] public int MaxBarsInTrade { get; set; } = 30;
    [NinjaScriptProperty] public int CooldownBars { get; set; } = 5;
    [NinjaScriptProperty] public bool EnableShorts { get; set; } = true;

    protected override void OnStateChange()
    {
        if (State == State.SetDefaults)
        {
            Name = "NQScalp_V1_EmaRibbonMomentum";
            Calculate = Calculate.OnBarClose;
            EntriesPerDirection = 1;
            DefaultQuantity = 1;
            IsExitOnSessionCloseStrategy = true;
        }
        else if (State == State.DataLoaded)
        {
            ema13 = EMA(Close, 13);
            ema21 = EMA(Close, 21);
            ema50 = EMA(Close, 50);
            macd  = MACD(Close, 12, 26, 9);
            rsi   = RSI(Close, 14, 3);
        }
    }

    protected override void OnBarUpdate()
    {
        if (CurrentBar < 55) return;

        // Time-based exit
        if (Position.MarketPosition != MarketPosition.Flat
            && BarsSinceEntryExecution() >= MaxBarsInTrade)
        {
            ExitLong("Timeout", "RibbonLong");
            ExitShort("Timeout", "RibbonShort");
            return;
        }

        if (CurrentBar - lastEntryBar < CooldownBars) return;

        bool emaStackUp   = ema13[0] > ema21[0] && ema21[0] > ema50[0];
        bool emaStackDown = ema13[0] < ema21[0] && ema21[0] < ema50[0];
        double hist0 = macd.Diff[0], hist1 = macd.Diff[1];
        bool histRising  = hist0 > 0 && hist0 > hist1;
        bool histFalling = hist0 < 0 && hist0 < hist1;
        double rsiVal = rsi[0];

        // LONG
        if (Position.MarketPosition == MarketPosition.Flat
            && emaStackUp && histRising && rsiVal >= 40 && rsiVal <= 70)
        {
            EnterLong(DefaultQuantity, "RibbonLong");
            SetProfitTarget("RibbonLong", CalculationMode.Ticks, ProfitTicks);
            SetStopLoss("RibbonLong", CalculationMode.Ticks, StopTicks, false);
            lastEntryBar = CurrentBar;
        }

        // SHORT
        if (EnableShorts && Position.MarketPosition == MarketPosition.Flat
            && emaStackDown && histFalling && rsiVal >= 30 && rsiVal <= 60)
        {
            EnterShort(DefaultQuantity, "RibbonShort");
            SetProfitTarget("RibbonShort", CalculationMode.Ticks, ProfitTicks);
            SetStopLoss("RibbonShort", CalculationMode.Ticks, StopTicks, false);
            lastEntryBar = CurrentBar;
        }
    }
}
```

**Key design decisions**
- *Histogram rising* (not just positive) filters out flat/fading momentum
- RSI 40-70 keeps entries in the momentum zone — above 70 tends to mean-revert quickly on NQ 1-min
- 5-bar cooldown prevents re-entering the same wave
- The 4:3 reward-to-risk ratio (16 tick target / 12 tick stop) gives you edge even at 45% win rate

Here is **Variant 2 — MACD Zero-Line Cross Scalp**.

**Logic summary**
- **Primary trigger**: MACD line crosses above 0 → long; crosses below 0 → short
- **EMA filter**: Close > EMA(21) > EMA(50) for longs (confirms medium-term trend)
- **RSI guard**: Long requires RSI 45-75, Short requires RSI 25-55
- **Discretionary exit**: If MACD reverses back through zero while in a trade, exit immediately

```csharp
public class NQScalp_V2_MacdZeroCross : Strategy
{
    private EMA ema13, ema21, ema50;
    private MACD macd;
    private RSI rsi;
    private int lastEntryBar = -100;

    [NinjaScriptProperty] public int ProfitTicks { get; set; } = 20;
    [NinjaScriptProperty] public int StopTicks { get; set; } = 12;
    [NinjaScriptProperty] public int CooldownBars { get; set; } = 8;

    protected override void OnStateChange()
    {
        if (State == State.SetDefaults)
        {
            Name = "NQScalp_V2_MacdZeroCross";
            Calculate = Calculate.OnBarClose;
            DefaultQuantity = 1;
        }
        else if (State == State.DataLoaded)
        {
            ema13 = EMA(Close, 13);
            ema21 = EMA(Close, 21);
            ema50 = EMA(Close, 50);
            macd  = MACD(Close, 12, 26, 9);
            rsi   = RSI(Close, 14, 3);
        }
    }

    protected override void OnBarUpdate()
    {
        if (CurrentBar < 55) return;

        // Exit: MACD reversal through zero
        if (Position.MarketPosition == MarketPosition.Long && macd.Value[0] < 0)
        { ExitLong("MACD Rev", "ZeroCrossLong"); return; }
        if (Position.MarketPosition == MarketPosition.Short && macd.Value[0] > 0)
        { ExitShort("MACD Rev", "ZeroCrossShort"); return; }

        if (CurrentBar - lastEntryBar < CooldownBars) return;

        bool macdCrossUp   = macd.Value[0] > 0 && macd.Value[1] <= 0;
        bool macdCrossDown = macd.Value[0] < 0 && macd.Value[1] >= 0;
        bool emaLong  = Close[0] > ema21[0] && ema21[0] > ema50[0];
        bool emaShort = Close[0] < ema21[0] && ema21[0] < ema50[0];
        double rsiVal = rsi[0];

        if (Position.MarketPosition == MarketPosition.Flat
            && macdCrossUp && emaLong && rsiVal > 45 && rsiVal < 75)
        {
            EnterLong(DefaultQuantity, "ZeroCrossLong");
            SetProfitTarget("ZeroCrossLong", CalculationMode.Ticks, ProfitTicks);
            SetStopLoss("ZeroCrossLong", CalculationMode.Ticks, StopTicks, false);
            lastEntryBar = CurrentBar;
        }

        if (Position.MarketPosition == MarketPosition.Flat
            && macdCrossDown && emaShort && rsiVal > 25 && rsiVal < 55)
        {
            EnterShort(DefaultQuantity, "ZeroCrossShort");
            SetProfitTarget("ZeroCrossShort", CalculationMode.Ticks, ProfitTicks);
            SetStopLoss("ZeroCrossShort", CalculationMode.Ticks, StopTicks, false);
            lastEntryBar = CurrentBar;
        }
    }
}
```

**Why this works for NQ scalping**
- Zero-line crosses are rarer than signal-line crosses, so they filter noise
- The EMA directional filter eliminates counter-trend traps
- The MACD-reversal exit cuts losses early when the signal fails
- 8-bar cooldown is longer because zero-line crosses are spaced further apart

Here is **Variant 3 — EMA Pullback Scalp**.

**Logic summary**
- **Trend**: EMA(13) > EMA(21) > EMA(50)
- **Pullback detection**: Price low touched or pierced EMA(21) within the last 2 bars (configurable tolerance)
- **Reclaim**: Current close is back above EMA(21)
- **MACD confirmation**: Histogram just flipped positive
- **RSI reload zone**: RSI(14) between 40-65 (pulled back enough to "reload" but not broken)
- **Exit**: Profit 20 ticks / Stop 10 ticks / EMA(13) < EMA(21) structure break

```csharp
protected override void OnBarUpdate()
{
    if (CurrentBar < 55) return;

    // Trailing structure-break exit
    if (Position.MarketPosition == MarketPosition.Long && ema13[0] < ema21[0])
    { ExitLong("EMA Break", "PullbackLong"); return; }

    if (CurrentBar - lastEntryBar < CooldownBars) return;

    bool uptrend      = ema13[0] > ema21[0] && ema21[0] > ema50[0];
    bool touchedEma21 = Low[0] <= ema21[0] + PullbackTolerance
                     || Low[1] <= ema21[1] + PullbackTolerance;
    bool reclaimed    = Close[0] > ema21[0];
    bool macdFlip     = macd.Diff[0] > 0;
    bool rsiReload    = rsi[0] >= 40 && rsi[0] <= 65;

    if (Position.MarketPosition == MarketPosition.Flat
        && uptrend && touchedEma21 && reclaimed && macdFlip && rsiReload)
    {
        EnterLong(DefaultQuantity, "PullbackLong");
        SetProfitTarget("PullbackLong", CalculationMode.Ticks, 20);
        SetStopLoss("PullbackLong", CalculationMode.Ticks, 10, false);
        lastEntryBar = CurrentBar;
    }
}
```

**Why pullback entries work on NQ**
- NQ trends intraday in 20-40 minute waves; the 21 EMA acts as dynamic support/resistance
- The "tolerance" parameter (default 1.0 pts) accounts for NQ's wide wicks without requiring an exact EMA touch
- Exiting on EMA 13 < 21 is a structure break — the fast trend is failing, get out
- The 2:1 reward-to-risk ratio (20 tick target / 10 tick stop) works because pullback entries have a natural edge in established trends

Here is **Variant 4 — RSI + MACD Hidden Divergence Scalp**.

**Concept**: Hidden divergence signals trend continuation. A bullish hidden divergence occurs when price makes a higher low while RSI makes a lower low — this means momentum is "coiling" for another leg up.

**Entry logic**
1. EMA stack confirms trend direction (13 > 21 > 50)
2. Scan back `LookbackBars` (default 10) to detect the divergence pattern
3. MACD histogram must be positive (momentum turning)
4. RSI between 35-60 (the reload zone after the dip)

```csharp
private bool BullishHiddenDivergence()
{
    double currPriceLow = Math.Min(Low[0], Low[1]);
    double currRsiLow   = Math.Min(rsi[0], rsi[1]);
    double priorPriceLow = double.MaxValue;
    double priorRsiLow   = double.MaxValue;

    for (int i = 2; i <= LookbackBars; i++)
    {
        if (Low[i] < priorPriceLow)
        {
            priorPriceLow = Low[i];
            priorRsiLow   = rsi[i];
        }
    }
    // Higher price low + lower RSI low = bullish hidden divergence
    return currPriceLow > priorPriceLow && currRsiLow < priorRsiLow;
}
```

**Trade management**
- Profit target: 24 ticks (6 pts) — wider because divergence entries have a strong edge
- Stop: 12 ticks (3 pts) — placed below the pullback low
- Structure exit: if EMA(13) crosses below EMA(21), the trend may be over

**Why hidden (not regular) divergence?**
- Regular divergence is a reversal signal — risky for scalping
- Hidden divergence is a *continuation* signal — you're trading with the trend, which has a statistical edge on NQ intraday

Here is **Variant 5 — Triple Confluence Scalp**, the highest-probability, lowest-frequency variant.

**Three independent layers must ALL be true:**

**Layer 1 — EMA (Trend)**
```
Close > EMA(13) > EMA(21) > EMA(50)
```
Price must be above *all* EMAs and the stack must be perfectly ordered. This is stricter than just 13 > 21 > 50.

**Layer 2 — MACD (Momentum)**
```
MACD line > 0 AND MACD line > Signal line AND Histogram > 0
```
All three MACD components must agree — line above zero, line above signal, and histogram positive.

**Layer 3 — RSI (Fresh momentum)**
```
RSI crossed above 50 within the last 3 bars AND current RSI between 50-70
```
The 50-cross requirement ensures you're catching *fresh* bullish momentum, not a stale overbought condition.

```csharp
private bool RsiCrossedAboveRecently(double level, int withinBars)
{
    for (int i = 0; i < withinBars; i++)
        if (rsi[i] > level && rsi[i + 1] <= level)
            return true;
    return false;
}

// In OnBarUpdate:
bool emaLong  = Close[0] > ema13[0] && ema13[0] > ema21[0] && ema21[0] > ema50[0];
bool macdLong = macd.Value[0] > 0 && macd.Value[0] > macd.Avg[0] && macd.Diff[0] > 0;
bool rsiLong  = RsiCrossedAboveRecently(50, 3) && rsi[0] >= 50 && rsi[0] <= 70;

if (emaLong && macdLong && rsiLong)
    EnterLong(DefaultQuantity, "ConfluenceLong");
```

**Trade management**
- Target: 28 ticks (7 pts) — wider target because triple-confluence setups have a high win rate
- Stop: 12 ticks (3 pts)
- Trailing exit: Close below EMA(13) → immediate exit
- Cooldown: 10 bars (these signals are rare, don't force re-entry)

**Why this is the most conservative**
- Requires agreement from trend (EMA), momentum (MACD), and strength (RSI) simultaneously
- The RSI 50-cross recency check ensures the signal is *fresh*, not stale
- Fewer trades, but each one has maximum confluence behind it
- Best suited for traders who prefer quality over quantity
